1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,280
 Hey everyone, Ryan here, and welcome back to our oral medicine series.

2
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:17,340
 This video will be all about INR, bleeding, clotting, and all of the concepts that go along

3
00:00:17,340 --> 00:00:20,120
 with those.

4
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:23,440
 So why is this important for us to discuss?

5
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:28,600
 Well beyond being a big topic on the board exam, a lot of procedures in dentistry cause

6
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:35,560
 bleeding, and usually that's not a problem, but in patients whose ability to control bleeding

7
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:41,760
 has been changed due to certain drugs or diseases, these same procedures could be potentially

8
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:43,560
 disastrous.

9
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:48,160
 So appropriate identification of these patients, and appropriate management of them while

10
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:52,880
 in the dental chair, will help mitigate those risks.

11
00:00:52,880 --> 00:01:00,520
 So in this context, patients will fall somewhere on this spectrum between bleeding and clotting.

12
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:06,000
 If they're on blood thinners, they're going to tend toward bleeding, and if they're older,

13
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,960
 had a major surgery or hospitalization or sedentary or immobile, they're going to

14
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:12,840
 tend toward clotting.

15
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:19,000
 So just keep this spectrum in mind throughout this video.

16
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:24,360
 Before we can get to INR, platelets, and all of that, first we need to talk about how clotting

17
00:01:24,360 --> 00:01:26,160
 happens.

18
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:32,200
 So let's say you get cut, and the blood vessels in that area are also injured as a result.

19
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:37,320
 Now to prevent your blood from just spilling out uncontrollably, there are four phases

20
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:40,200
 of the hemostasis process.

21
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:42,840
 This means stopping blood.

22
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:49,000
 The first phase is vascular, this involves vasoconstriction, where the affected vessels

23
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:51,800
 physically contract.

24
00:01:51,800 --> 00:01:58,080
 Next is the platelet phase, which involves a platelet plug, where platelets rush to the

25
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:03,600
 area, and this is considered primary hemostasis.

26
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:09,400
 Next the third phase is coagulation, which involves a fibrin clot.

27
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:15,240
 It's this mesh of fibrin material that basically holds the platelet plug together.

28
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:19,480
 This is considered secondary hemostasis.

29
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:22,280
 And then finally we have the fibrinolytic phase.

30
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:28,480
 It involves plasmin, which is an enzyme in blood, that cuts the fibrin mesh and dissolves

31
00:02:28,480 --> 00:02:31,520
 the clot after it's done its job.

32
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:38,080
 So primary hemostasis is all about platelets, secondary hemostasis is all about coagulation.

33
00:02:38,080 --> 00:02:43,240
 Note that these are two entirely different things.

34
00:02:43,240 --> 00:02:50,760
 So hemostasis as a whole relies on the proper function of blood vessels, platelets, and

35
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:57,480
 coagulation factors, all of which we'll talk more about.

36
00:02:57,480 --> 00:03:03,440
 Bleeding disorders can be inherited via genetics or acquired via the environment.

37
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:07,280
 And there are hundreds of bleeding disorders that we could talk about, but of course that

38
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:11,940
 would take a really long time, and it's just beyond the scope of the dental board exam.

39
00:03:11,940 --> 00:03:15,320
 So I'm going to focus on only the important ones.

40
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:21,000
 The vascular phase, as we just talked about, is our first step in hemostasis.

41
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,840
 So we will properly start with vascular wall defects.

42
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:32,160
 Thankfully, this is not a big deal for dental care, and patients with these defects rarely

43
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:37,760
 will have a severe bleeding episode after a tooth extraction.

44
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:44,060
 Marfan syndrome and Ehlers-Danlos syndrome affect connective tissue, which in turn affects

45
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:45,960
 the vascular wall.

46
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:53,440
 And then Ossler-Weberrendu syndrome, also known as hereditary hemorrhagic telangectasia,

47
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:56,160
 affects blood vessels more directly.

48
00:03:56,160 --> 00:04:02,140
 So all three of these can compromise the integrity of the vascular wall, impacting that first

49
00:04:02,140 --> 00:04:05,920
 phase of hemostasis.

50
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:12,120
 Management of these patients includes coordination with their hematologist, minimally invasive

51
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:17,620
 dentistry with good surgical technique, local hemostatic measures, which I'll talk more

52
00:04:17,620 --> 00:04:25,320
 about in depth later in the video, and avoiding aspirin and other nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory

53
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:26,320
 drugs.

54
00:04:26,320 --> 00:04:30,400
 We'll talk more about those later as well.

55
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:36,520
 The platelet pathway is the next phase of hemostasis, so let's talk about that one next.

56
00:04:36,520 --> 00:04:44,140
 So how this works is we have an endothelium layer that lines the inside of each blood vessel,

57
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:52,280
 and endothelial damage causes the release of von Willebrande factor, VWF for short, from

58
00:04:52,280 --> 00:05:02,400
 the endothelium, which then attaches to circulating platelets in purple, via glycoprotein 1B.

59
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:09,240
 So at this stage, the platelets are now adhered to the endothelium via this glycoprotein and

60
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:10,680
 von Willebrande factor.

61
00:05:10,680 --> 00:05:15,120
 So that's the first step, adhesion.

62
00:05:15,120 --> 00:05:16,960
 Next comes activation.

63
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:23,160
 So binding to the von Willebrande factor really excites these platelets, and so they get activated

64
00:05:23,160 --> 00:05:25,400
 and grow these extensions.

65
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:31,720
 Not only that, but they secrete activators that include thromboxane A2 and adenosine

66
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:39,280
 diphosphate ADP, which further activate other circulating platelets, so it's this positive

67
00:05:39,280 --> 00:05:43,600
 feedback loop effect.

68
00:05:43,600 --> 00:05:50,960
 And now that the platelets are activated, they begin to express glycoprotein 2B/3A.

69
00:05:50,960 --> 00:05:57,080
 Fibrinogen is also floating through the bloodstream and sticks to this glycoprotein complex like

70
00:05:57,080 --> 00:06:04,400
 Velcro, and since Velcro is super sticky, the platelets begin sticking to each other,

71
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:06,640
 which is called aggregation.

72
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:13,200
 So keep in mind that as more and more platelets aggregate to each other, they also get activated,

73
00:06:13,200 --> 00:06:20,160
 releasing more thromboxane A2 and ADP, and the positive feedback loop continues.

74
00:06:20,160 --> 00:06:26,960
 Now I remember this step because aggregation, the word, has two of the same letters right

75
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:32,640
 next to each other to symbolize two platelets sticking to each other.

76
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:38,020
 The end result of primary hemostasis is a platelet plug composed of a whole bunch of

77
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:46,040
 platelets, fibrinogen and tethered to the endothelium via von Willebrand factor.

78
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:51,440
 So that's how it's supposed to work, but how can it go wrong?

79
00:06:51,440 --> 00:06:58,320
 Well we have what's known as von Willebrand disease, which causes a deficiency in von

80
00:06:58,320 --> 00:07:05,960
 Willebrand factor, affecting the ability of platelets to adhere to the vessel wall.

81
00:07:05,960 --> 00:07:12,560
 Now it's the most common congenital bleeding disorder affecting about 1% of the population.

82
00:07:12,560 --> 00:07:21,920
 So without that lime green starburst here, that initial step of adhesion cannot happen.

83
00:07:21,920 --> 00:07:28,640
 And we didn't talk about this, but von Willebrand factor also acts as a carrier for coagulation

84
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:30,200
 factor 8.

85
00:07:30,200 --> 00:07:34,360
 We'll get more into coagulation a little bit later, but just keep that in the back of your

86
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:38,720
 head that this condition affects both platelets and coagulation.

87
00:07:38,720 --> 00:07:41,480
 So we'll see it again real soon.

88
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:46,800
 And we also have thrombocytopenia, this refers to low platelets.

89
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:54,320
 It's not technically a diagnosis, but rather the result of another disease or etiology.

90
00:07:54,320 --> 00:08:01,640
 It can be drug induced, like drugs that are toxic to the bone marrow, or immune mediated.

91
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:07,160
 So it could be related to leukemia or HIV, for example.

92
00:08:07,160 --> 00:08:14,560
 So a normal platelet count is somewhere between 150,000 to 450,000 platelets per microliter

93
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:16,240
 of blood.

94
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:20,600
 Less than this is considered thrombocytopenia.

95
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:26,040
 You have increased clinical bleeding when that's below 50,000, and then you can have

96
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:31,400
 spontaneous bleeding once that number hits below 20,000.

97
00:08:31,400 --> 00:08:36,040
 And this is an example of what a platelet looks like compared to a bunch of red blood

98
00:08:36,040 --> 00:08:40,320
 cells.

99
00:08:40,320 --> 00:08:44,840
 The platelet pathway can also be disrupted on purpose.

100
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:50,640
 So patients may be treated with anti-platelet medications if they had a history of heart

101
00:08:50,640 --> 00:08:57,480
 attack or placement of a coronary artery stent in order to prevent formation of unwanted

102
00:08:57,480 --> 00:08:59,200
 platelet plugs.

103
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:07,520
 So, these medications are designed to interfere with one of those three A's, adhesion, activation,

104
00:09:07,520 --> 00:09:09,960
 or aggregation.

105
00:09:09,960 --> 00:09:17,880
 So aspirin inhibits the Cox-1 enzyme to prevent synthesis of thromboxane A2 in order

106
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:22,400
 to block activation, and this is irreversible.

107
00:09:22,400 --> 00:09:29,240
 Now other NSAIDs, like ibuprofen, do the same thing but are reversible.

108
00:09:29,240 --> 00:09:38,240
 Clopidogrel is a drug that competes with ADP at its receptor to block activation.

109
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:45,800
 And F6imab binds to the glycoprotein 2B3A complex to block aggregation.

110
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:52,960
 I remember this one because A and B are right next to each other in the drug name and so

111
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:58,080
 are B and A in this glycoprotein complex.

112
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:05,560
 So these are three examples of drugs that can disrupt the platelet pathway.

113
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:07,920
 And then we come to platelet testing.

114
00:10:07,920 --> 00:10:09,920
 So why is this done?

115
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:17,800
 Well, a hematologist may want to run one of these tests in order to diagnose von Willebrand

116
00:10:17,800 --> 00:10:24,760
 disease, or to check the status of thrombocytopenia, or maybe even to see how anti-platelet medications

117
00:10:24,760 --> 00:10:28,600
 are working to name some examples.

118
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:34,840
 And next we have to ask, well how do we test for something like platelet number, quantity,

119
00:10:34,840 --> 00:10:37,720
 or platelet function, quality?

120
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:43,720
 Well we can order a platelet count, that's where you have a blood sample taken, and as

121
00:10:43,720 --> 00:10:50,320
 part of a complete blood count, the concentration of platelets in the blood sample is counted.

122
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:55,680
 So thrombocytopenia would be confirmed if platelets are low, again it tends to be common

123
00:10:55,680 --> 00:10:58,720
 in HIV or leukemia patients.

124
00:10:58,720 --> 00:11:03,360
 So this would of course be talking about quantity of platelets.

125
00:11:03,360 --> 00:11:09,400
 An IV bleeding test involves making a cut in the patient's arm and then timing how long

126
00:11:09,400 --> 00:11:12,600
 it takes for bleeding to stop.

127
00:11:12,600 --> 00:11:17,840
 It has been used to screen for disorders of platelet function, like in the use of chronic

128
00:11:17,840 --> 00:11:24,960
 NSAIDs, and platelet number, again thrombocytopenia, so it can be used to test either quantity

129
00:11:24,960 --> 00:11:26,520
 or quality.

130
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:32,280
 Unfortunately, it's rather unreliable and no longer used.

131
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:36,920
 The peripheral blood smear involves looking under a microscope to see whether the cells

132
00:11:36,920 --> 00:11:42,560
 look morphologically normal or not, so this will tell you about platelet quality.

133
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:47,960
 The platelet aggregation test checks how well your platelets clump together to form that

134
00:11:47,960 --> 00:11:48,960
 plug.

135
00:11:48,960 --> 00:11:54,840
 And then the platelet function analyzer, the PFA 100, is an instrument that measures platelet

136
00:11:54,840 --> 00:12:00,480
 dependent coagulation under flow conditions, so it's better than the bleeding test but

137
00:12:00,480 --> 00:12:06,000
 unfortunately it's not sensitive enough to rule out mild bleeding disorders, so neither

138
00:12:06,000 --> 00:12:14,920
 the bleeding test nor PFA 100 are recommended as screening tests to be used by dentists.

139
00:12:14,920 --> 00:12:19,760
 So patient considerations for platelet disorders are the same as they were for those vascular

140
00:12:19,760 --> 00:12:23,480
 wall defects.

141
00:12:23,480 --> 00:12:28,400
 And now we come to phase three of hemostasis, coagulation.

142
00:12:28,400 --> 00:12:34,640
 So this is a table I put together for each coagulation factor in the coagulation cascade

143
00:12:34,640 --> 00:12:38,760
 or pathway and where they're found in the body.

144
00:12:38,760 --> 00:12:43,480
 We don't have time to talk through all of this but you can always refer back to it later.

145
00:12:43,480 --> 00:12:48,080
 I thought it might be helpful to just have all this information clearly laid out.

146
00:12:48,080 --> 00:12:57,240
 And note that a vast majority of these coagulation factors are synthesized in the liver.

147
00:12:57,240 --> 00:13:01,880
 So here I have listed the factors involved by number.

148
00:13:01,880 --> 00:13:08,120
 So how this works is each factor activates the factor below it.

149
00:13:08,120 --> 00:13:20,600
 So for example, 12 would make 11, factor 11 turn into factor 11A, A for activated.

150
00:13:20,600 --> 00:13:28,400
 And 11A would in turn make nine turn into nine A and so on.

151
00:13:28,400 --> 00:13:31,440
 So you'd go down the cascade in that fashion.

152
00:13:31,440 --> 00:13:37,400
 I don't have the A's listed because it just makes things way more complicated and busy

153
00:13:37,400 --> 00:13:39,600
 than they have to be.

154
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:45,360
 So let's actually start at the bottom or the end of this coagulation cascade.

155
00:13:45,360 --> 00:13:53,480
 So the clot is obviously the goal here and factor one is at the end of this cascade.

156
00:13:53,480 --> 00:13:57,320
 And it's aptly named because it is number one.

157
00:13:57,320 --> 00:14:01,280
 It's the most important factor of this pathway.

158
00:14:01,280 --> 00:14:06,360
 If you go back to our table, one is fibrinogen.

159
00:14:06,360 --> 00:14:09,480
 And fibrinogen may sound familiar.

160
00:14:09,480 --> 00:14:13,720
 Well that's because fibrinogen is also in the platelet pathway.

161
00:14:13,720 --> 00:14:19,280
 It was the Velcro responsible for aggregation of platelets to each other.

162
00:14:19,280 --> 00:14:26,560
 So you may be wondering how does the body know where to activate this coagulation cascade?

163
00:14:26,560 --> 00:14:33,760
 The answer is at the giant plug of platelets coated in a mesh of fibrinogen, the substrate

164
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:36,920
 for this entire cascade.

165
00:14:36,920 --> 00:14:41,360
 And this is why our blood isn't just one giant clot.

166
00:14:41,360 --> 00:14:46,560
 It only clots where we already have a platelet plug.

167
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:47,920
 Very very cool.

168
00:14:47,920 --> 00:14:50,560
 So we have two separate pathways to start.

169
00:14:50,560 --> 00:14:58,600
 We have intrinsic in red and extrinsic in green that converge on the common pathway in blue,

170
00:14:58,600 --> 00:15:01,320
 which is shared between the two.

171
00:15:01,320 --> 00:15:09,520
 And lastly for memory sake, factor ten or the roman numeral X is in the central location

172
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:14,600
 of all of these pathways because of course, if you've ever looked for berry treasure,

173
00:15:14,600 --> 00:15:16,920
 X marks the spot.

174
00:15:16,920 --> 00:15:20,320
 So let's start with the extrinsic pathway.

175
00:15:20,320 --> 00:15:23,440
 This is outside the blood vessel.

176
00:15:23,440 --> 00:15:29,440
 It's triggered by external trauma that causes blood to escape its vessel.

177
00:15:29,440 --> 00:15:35,240
 And the damaged endothelium expresses tissue factor or thromboplastin.

178
00:15:35,240 --> 00:15:42,440
 That's just another name for factor three, which jump starts this pathway.

179
00:15:42,440 --> 00:15:48,240
 It's usually quicker than the intrinsic pathway as a side note.

180
00:15:48,240 --> 00:15:55,720
 So three activates seven, which activates ten, and now we're back in the common pathway.

181
00:15:55,720 --> 00:16:05,980
 So ten works with five to activate factor two, which activates factor one and thirteen.

182
00:16:05,980 --> 00:16:10,800
 You can remember this because one is the smallest and thirteen is the largest number.

183
00:16:10,800 --> 00:16:13,440
 So those two work together.

184
00:16:13,440 --> 00:16:18,120
 Now we've talked at length about factor one, but now let's spend some time on factor two

185
00:16:18,120 --> 00:16:22,680
 because well, it's the second most important factor in this list.

186
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:25,040
 It's called prothrombin.

187
00:16:25,040 --> 00:16:32,720
 And when activated to factor two A, it becomes thrombin, which activates a bunch of other

188
00:16:32,720 --> 00:16:33,720
 factors.

189
00:16:33,720 --> 00:16:46,080
 It actually activates five and seven and eight, eleven, and even thirteen.

190
00:16:46,080 --> 00:16:52,800
 Providing a positive feedback loop and even activating the intrinsic pathway up here.

191
00:16:52,800 --> 00:16:59,560
 So when factor two gets involved, it's really speeding everything up.

192
00:16:59,560 --> 00:17:01,760
 Now let's talk about the intrinsic pathway.

193
00:17:01,760 --> 00:17:07,200
 This is inside the blood vessel, and you may be wondering, well, what's the deal with

194
00:17:07,200 --> 00:17:10,800
 factor twelve, also called hagmin factor?

195
00:17:10,800 --> 00:17:13,360
 It's not one of the ones activated by thrombin.

196
00:17:13,360 --> 00:17:17,080
 Remember, thrombin only reached up to factor eleven.

197
00:17:17,080 --> 00:17:20,480
 So what is factor twelve doing way up here?

198
00:17:20,480 --> 00:17:23,600
 And how does it get activated initially?

199
00:17:23,600 --> 00:17:30,800
 Well, trauma can expose collagen on the inside of blood vessels, which activates factor twelve.

200
00:17:30,800 --> 00:17:35,360
 But it's not actually necessary for clotting to occur, because again, thrombin can start

201
00:17:35,360 --> 00:17:40,000
 this pathway from factor eleven, and then it goes from there.

202
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,560
 What's nice is there is some order to this pathway.

203
00:17:42,560 --> 00:17:48,680
 We start with twelve, then go to eleven, we skip ten and go down to nine, which is next

204
00:17:48,680 --> 00:17:52,280
 to eight, and it lines up with seven over here.

205
00:17:52,280 --> 00:17:58,920
 So there's some order in terms of counting down with numbers right next to each other.

206
00:17:58,920 --> 00:18:04,480
 But of course, like with the platelet pathway, things can go wrong with the coagulation cascade.

207
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:07,800
 So let's talk about some clotting factor defects.

208
00:18:07,800 --> 00:18:15,400
 So for inherited defects, we have, once again, as promised, Von Willebrand disease.

209
00:18:15,400 --> 00:18:21,440
 So remember, as I mentioned before, Von Willebrand disease not only messes up platelets, but it

210
00:18:21,440 --> 00:18:28,720
 also interferes with factor eight, so we have it on this slide as well.

211
00:18:28,720 --> 00:18:34,840
 Next we have to talk about another big disease, hemophilia, which comes from the Greek meaning

212
00:18:34,840 --> 00:18:37,400
 love to bleed.

213
00:18:37,400 --> 00:18:43,760
 Now these people have a hereditary condition where they bleed easily because they are deficient

214
00:18:43,760 --> 00:18:47,040
 in a certain clotting factor.

215
00:18:47,040 --> 00:18:53,720
 Hemophilia A is by far the most common of the three hemophilias here, and it's autosomal

216
00:18:53,720 --> 00:18:59,240
 X-linked recessive, therefore affecting mainly males.

217
00:18:59,240 --> 00:19:05,960
 Also if local anesthesia is necessary for a patient with hemophilia A, there is an eighty

218
00:19:05,960 --> 00:19:13,560
 percent chance that a patient with hemophilia A will develop a hematoma following an inferior

219
00:19:13,560 --> 00:19:20,000
 alveolar nerve block injection without prior factor eight infusion.

220
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:26,640
 The hematoma could even be fatal if it accumulates in the media stinum and compromises the airway.

221
00:19:26,640 --> 00:19:34,720
 So bottom line, stick with local infiltrations only in the mandible for these patients.

222
00:19:34,720 --> 00:19:43,680
 So hemophilia A involves a deficiency in factor eight, A for eight, and then hemophilia B involves

223
00:19:43,680 --> 00:19:50,200
 a deficiency in factor nine, hemophilia C involves a deficiency in factor eleven.

224
00:19:50,200 --> 00:19:54,800
 And these two, you can remember go together, calling nine one one.

225
00:19:54,800 --> 00:19:57,280
 So that's how I remember those.

226
00:19:57,280 --> 00:20:03,000
 Also notice how all forms of hemophilia affect the intrinsic pathway.

227
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:08,080
 We have eight nine and eleven, which are all right next to each other in that intrinsic

228
00:20:08,080 --> 00:20:09,800
 pathway.

229
00:20:09,800 --> 00:20:14,400
 So that can also help you remember those.

230
00:20:14,400 --> 00:20:20,720
 And then to finish off this list, we have two acquired disease processes.

231
00:20:20,720 --> 00:20:28,520
 So vitamin K deficiency is the most common of all acquired coagulation disorders.

232
00:20:28,520 --> 00:20:37,960
 Those two seven nine and ten are made in the liver and require vitamin K for their synthesis.

233
00:20:37,960 --> 00:20:44,960
 So that's why they're specifically impacted if vitamin K is low.

234
00:20:44,960 --> 00:20:50,960
 Liver dysfunction, like severe liver disease, can lead to deficiencies again in these vitamin

235
00:20:50,960 --> 00:20:56,200
 K dependent coagulation factors, two seven nine and ten.

236
00:20:56,200 --> 00:21:01,000
 I purposely listed some in number form and some in romanumeral form because they can

237
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:04,300
 be listed in either form on the board exam.

238
00:21:04,300 --> 00:21:07,960
 So just get used to seeing them in both ways.

239
00:21:07,960 --> 00:21:14,000
 There are other disease processes that we can put on this list like chronic kidney disease

240
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:19,480
 and autoimmune diseases, but these are the main ones that I wanted to cover that are

241
00:21:19,480 --> 00:21:24,880
 most likely to appear on your board exam.

242
00:21:24,880 --> 00:21:31,440
 Now just like with the platelet pathway, we have medications that can disrupt the coagulation

243
00:21:31,440 --> 00:21:34,560
 cascade on purpose.

244
00:21:34,560 --> 00:21:41,600
 So if you have someone who is a cloder and dangerously so, then one of these may be prescribed

245
00:21:41,600 --> 00:21:43,040
 for that reason.

246
00:21:43,040 --> 00:21:48,640
 Some examples include reducing the risk of heart attack or stroke, patients with atrial

247
00:21:48,640 --> 00:21:55,300
 fibrillation, prosthetic heart valves, history of deep venous thrombosis blood clots and

248
00:21:55,300 --> 00:21:57,480
 things like that.

249
00:21:57,480 --> 00:22:05,680
 Now the next point I want to make absolutely crystal clear, anti-coagulants are different

250
00:22:05,680 --> 00:22:13,680
 from anti-platelet medications that we talked about before because these drugs affect coagulation

251
00:22:13,680 --> 00:22:17,080
 not the platelet pathway.

252
00:22:17,080 --> 00:22:25,000
 For example, a ton of people refer to aspirin as an anti-coagulant, and that's not correct.

253
00:22:25,000 --> 00:22:31,080
 Aspirin inhibits platelet activation by blocking Cox-1 and thromboxian A2 synthesis.

254
00:22:31,080 --> 00:22:37,640
 Now while it is a blood thinner, aspirin does not affect the coagulation cascade at all,

255
00:22:37,640 --> 00:22:40,560
 and so it's not an anti-coagulant.

256
00:22:40,560 --> 00:22:46,360
 I threw out the category blood thinner, so both anti-platelets and anti-coagulants are

257
00:22:46,360 --> 00:22:50,840
 blood thinners, they make someone more likely to bleed.

258
00:22:50,840 --> 00:22:54,680
 Now here are the anti-coagulants.

259
00:22:54,680 --> 00:22:59,960
 So warfarin blocks reduction recycling of vitamin K, which means that, once again, those

260
00:22:59,960 --> 00:23:06,000
 factors that depend on vitamin K, 2, 7, 9, and 10 are hindered, which will definitely

261
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:09,040
 mess with clotting.

262
00:23:09,040 --> 00:23:17,040
 Thrombin pulls thrombin and this thing called anti-thrombin together, which blocks factor

263
00:23:17,040 --> 00:23:18,040
 2 specifically.

264
00:23:18,040 --> 00:23:27,520
 Epixaban, or Eliquis, directly inhibits factor 10A, and then Dabogatran binds to thrombin,

265
00:23:27,520 --> 00:23:34,640
 which is factor 2A, at its active site, and inhibits it, so this is a direct thrombin

266
00:23:34,640 --> 00:23:45,520
 inhibitor, while the other three examples are indirect thrombin inhibitors.

267
00:23:45,520 --> 00:23:51,680
 Now like in the platelet section, there are tests that a hematologist can order to check

268
00:23:51,680 --> 00:23:56,040
 on how this coagulation cascade is working.

269
00:23:56,040 --> 00:24:03,120
 So let's talk about the activated partial thromboplastin time test first, or APTT.

270
00:24:03,120 --> 00:24:09,280
 This measures the number of seconds it takes for a clot to form in a sample of blood after

271
00:24:09,280 --> 00:24:11,920
 certain reagents are added to it.

272
00:24:11,920 --> 00:24:19,440
 It's used to check the intrinsic system and common pathway, so it's also the best single

273
00:24:19,440 --> 00:24:24,160
 screening test for coagulation disorders.

274
00:24:24,160 --> 00:24:30,080
 It's used to test Von Willebrand disease, hemophilia, among many others.

275
00:24:30,080 --> 00:24:35,440
 The normal range for this test is somewhere between 25 and 35 seconds.

276
00:24:35,440 --> 00:24:40,680
 A shorter time means they are more towards the clotting end of the spectrum.

277
00:24:40,680 --> 00:24:47,760
 A longer time means they are closer to the bleeding end of the spectrum.

278
00:24:47,760 --> 00:24:52,840
 And then we have the prothrombin time test, or PT test.

279
00:24:52,840 --> 00:24:57,680
 This one also measures how long it takes for a clot to form in a blood sample, but it's

280
00:24:57,680 --> 00:25:05,900
 used almost exclusively for patients on Warfarin and is used to derive the INR, which is the

281
00:25:05,900 --> 00:25:11,600
 international normalized ratio for a particular patient.

282
00:25:11,600 --> 00:25:16,040
 All INR is a normalized PT.

283
00:25:16,040 --> 00:25:21,200
 It's normalized for different lab materials from lab to lab.

284
00:25:21,200 --> 00:25:27,360
 And how to remember that they are for patients on Warfarin is because the last three letters

285
00:25:27,360 --> 00:25:33,060
 of the word Warfarin are INR, just in a different order.

286
00:25:33,060 --> 00:25:39,840
 So the normal range for this test is somewhere between 11 and 15 seconds.

287
00:25:39,840 --> 00:25:49,840
 The smaller the INR, that's indicating a clotter, the larger the INR that's indicating a bleeder.

288
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:56,520
 And just as an aside, the factors in red here are those vitamin K dependent factors that

289
00:25:56,520 --> 00:26:00,760
 we talked about in this slide and this slide.

290
00:26:00,760 --> 00:26:06,440
 So just to help you remember two, seven, nine, and ten, the nice thing is they're all right

291
00:26:06,440 --> 00:26:11,400
 next to each other in that handy diagram.

292
00:26:11,400 --> 00:26:17,440
 Alright the moment you've all been waiting for, let's dive more into INR.

293
00:26:17,440 --> 00:26:21,760
 And this is a huge, huge part of the new board exam.

294
00:26:21,760 --> 00:26:27,440
 So first, for all patients taking Warfarin, there are some precautions that should be

295
00:26:27,440 --> 00:26:28,800
 taken.

296
00:26:28,800 --> 00:26:35,080
 We want to avoid aspirin and all other NSAIDs, which can make bleeding risk higher.

297
00:26:35,080 --> 00:26:38,560
 And we know now that's by messing with platelets.

298
00:26:38,560 --> 00:26:45,640
 So Tylenol with or without codeine is suggested for pain control instead.

299
00:26:45,640 --> 00:26:53,480
 We want to avoid metronidazol, erythromycin, and broad-spectrum antibiotics that can potentiate

300
00:26:53,480 --> 00:26:56,960
 the anti-coagulation effect of Warfarin.

301
00:26:56,960 --> 00:27:05,400
 Similarly, herbal supplements can make anti-coagulation more significant as well, can enhance the bleeding

302
00:27:05,400 --> 00:27:07,560
 for that patient.

303
00:27:07,560 --> 00:27:14,120
 Barbiturates and steroids actually antagonize the action of Warfarin.

304
00:27:14,120 --> 00:27:20,320
 So those should be avoided in order to prevent the INR from getting too low toward the clotting

305
00:27:20,320 --> 00:27:22,160
 side.

306
00:27:22,160 --> 00:27:29,000
 For Warfarin patients undergoing surgery, local hemostatic measures should be taken.

307
00:27:29,000 --> 00:27:32,560
 So this is what I was mentioning earlier on in the video.

308
00:27:32,560 --> 00:27:38,480
 And these involve compressive packing and dressing to physically stop bleeding, extra

309
00:27:38,480 --> 00:27:46,400
 sutures and primary closure over the surgical site were possible, a 4.8% tranexamic acid

310
00:27:46,400 --> 00:27:52,800
 mouthwash which promotes clotting and topical thrombin which does the same.

311
00:27:52,800 --> 00:27:59,280
 So clinical evidence suggests that the risk of bleeding out with a therapeutic INR is

312
00:27:59,280 --> 00:28:01,120
 very, very small.

313
00:28:01,120 --> 00:28:05,080
 In general, the risk of clotting outweighs the benefit.

314
00:28:05,080 --> 00:28:10,960
 In other words, it's better to stay on those medications that favor the propensity to bleed

315
00:28:10,960 --> 00:28:16,600
 rather than the propensity to clot without them which can be life threatening for certain

316
00:28:16,600 --> 00:28:18,160
 patients.

317
00:28:18,160 --> 00:28:25,280
 However, if a complex major oral surgery is planned where significant bleeding is likely,

318
00:28:25,280 --> 00:28:31,320
 only then would modification of Warfarin therapy with a medical consultation be potentially

319
00:28:31,320 --> 00:28:33,120
 necessary.

320
00:28:33,120 --> 00:28:36,520
 So where exactly do we make that threshold?

321
00:28:36,520 --> 00:28:38,960
 You might be wondering.

322
00:28:38,960 --> 00:28:46,840
 So for an INR between 2 and 3, this is actually a great therapeutic range for many conditions

323
00:28:46,840 --> 00:28:52,600
 like atrial fibrillation, deep venous thrombosis and pulmonary embolism.

324
00:28:52,600 --> 00:28:57,080
 Generally, there are no bleeding concerns for patients in this range.

325
00:28:57,080 --> 00:29:01,640
 And beyond the above considerations that we just talked about, you can proceed with

326
00:29:01,640 --> 00:29:04,300
 any dental treatment.

327
00:29:04,300 --> 00:29:12,080
 For an INR between 3 and 3.5, it may be desired for patients with a prosthetic heart valve,

328
00:29:12,080 --> 00:29:16,020
 but it's a bit high for major complex surgeries.

329
00:29:16,020 --> 00:29:22,640
 So if the patient is undergoing a simple minor oral surgery like a single tooth extraction,

330
00:29:22,640 --> 00:29:26,360
 you can continue with treatment with the above considerations.

331
00:29:26,360 --> 00:29:32,520
 But if it's a major oral surgery like a full arch extraction, then we want to defer treatment

332
00:29:32,520 --> 00:29:39,240
 and refer to their medical doctor about changing the Warfarin dose temporarily and then retesting

333
00:29:39,240 --> 00:29:44,120
 INR before proceeding with dental care.

334
00:29:44,120 --> 00:29:51,200
 Now if the INR is greater than 3.5, no matter what is planned, the dentist should refer the

335
00:29:51,200 --> 00:29:56,760
 patient to their doctor and request that the dosage be reduced to allow that INR to fall

336
00:29:56,760 --> 00:29:59,440
 within a healthier therapeutic range.

337
00:29:59,440 --> 00:30:05,280
 If the dose is reduced by the physician, it will take 3-5 days for the desired reduction

338
00:30:05,280 --> 00:30:06,560
 to occur.

339
00:30:06,560 --> 00:30:12,400
 The reduction should be confirmed by a new INR before the dental or surgical procedure,

340
00:30:12,400 --> 00:30:17,960
 which should be scheduled within 2 days after that new INR.

341
00:30:17,960 --> 00:30:21,960
 After the procedure is finished, and there are no complications in terms of bleeding

342
00:30:21,960 --> 00:30:29,080
 or infection, then the physician should be contacted to resume the usual Warfarin dose.

343
00:30:29,080 --> 00:30:34,120
 And at the end of the day, if you're ever unsure about any of these steps, consult with

344
00:30:34,120 --> 00:30:41,840
 the patient's hematologist to ensure a safe tooth extraction.

345
00:30:41,840 --> 00:30:46,880
 Lastly oral manifestations of people with any of the above that we just talked about, vascular

346
00:30:46,880 --> 00:30:52,360
 wall defects, platelet disorders, or coagulation issues.

347
00:30:52,360 --> 00:30:57,360
 So what we can see is easy or spontaneous gingerable bleeding.

348
00:30:57,360 --> 00:31:04,440
 Oral tissues like the soft palate, tongue, and buccomucosa may show these little peteciae

349
00:31:04,440 --> 00:31:12,600
 or maybe a larger echomoses, sometimes even you see jaundice, pallor, and ulcers.

350
00:31:12,600 --> 00:31:18,440
 And lastly, hemorthrosis of the TMJ, that's bleeding into the jaw joint.

351
00:31:18,440 --> 00:31:26,880
 This is a rare finding, but has been linked specifically to hemophilia.

